; tc.macro - tool change handler for grblHAL.
;
; Replaces the firmware's built-in M6 flow. Triggered automatically when
; /tc.macro is present at the SD card root and the SDcard plugin attaches
; (boot or after $FM). With this in place, $341/$342/$343/$345/$346 are
; ignored — all tool change behavior is defined here.
;
; Flow:
;   - Spindle/coolant off, lift Z to safe (machine -5).
;   - For real swaps (selected_tool != 8): rapid to G30 (foam bin),
;     prompt operator, M0 hold for cycle start.
;   - For T8 (sentinel = probe already in spindle): skip swap.
;   - Rapid to G59.3 origin (over puck / touch-plate block).
;   - Probe: seek down at F500, pull off 2mm, slow re-touch at F25.
;     Toolsetter mode: G38.2 auto-routes to the toolsetter input because XY
;     is at G59.3 within TOOLSETTER_RADIUS (motion_control.c:1018-1019).
;     Touch-plate mode: always the MAIN probe input (see _tc_touchplate below).
;   - First call of session (#<_tlo_ref> == 0): store probe machine-Z as
;     the reference; no G43.1 applied.
;   - Subsequent calls: G43.1 Z[probe_z - tlo_ref] applies dynamic TLO.
;   - Park at G30.
;
; Required state:
;   - Machine is homed.
;   - G30 is set to the tool-swap position (over foam bin, near max Z).
;   - G59.3 is set to the approach position over whichever TLO target is in
;     use (toolsetter puck, or a fixed touch-plate block - see _tc_touchplate
;     below), XY centered, Z high enough that the longest tool clears it
;     during the XY rapid in.
;   - #<_tlo_ref> defaults to 0 the first time this macro runs in a session if
;     nothing else has set it yet (guarded below with EXISTS[] - Setup's own
;     generated program also loads a baseline into it, but a tool change run
;     standalone on a truly cold session, before Setup ever runs, must not
;     error trying to read an undefined named parameter).
;
; #<_tc_touchplate> (set below, 2026-07-31): 0 = toolsetter puck (default,
; original behavior - mechanical NC switch on the TOOLSETTER input, only
; rigid cutting tools use it, T8's 3D-probe stylus always uses the main
; input). 1 = fixed touch-plate block instead - no toolsetter hardware at
; all, so EVERY tool (T8's stylus included) probes via the MAIN probe input.
; A plain metal tool tip closes the circuit against the plate the same way
; stock-edge touch-plate probing does (Start Job's Touch Plate probe type) -
; this relies on the TOOL being conductive (true for essentially any
; carbide/HSS cutting tool), not the stock, so it works regardless of the
; job's Material setting. The TLO math itself is unaffected either way: the
; block's exact thickness/position never appears in the calculation below -
; only the DELTA between the reference tool's probed Z and each subsequent
; tool's probed Z matters (same reasoning that makes the puck's own exact
; height irrelevant), so a flat block works as a TLO target with zero
; changes beyond skipping the toolsetter-input switch.
#<_tc_touchplate> = 0
;
; Firmware-supplied NGC params:
;   #<_current_tool>  - currently mounted tool ID
;   #<_selected_tool> - T value from M6 T<n>

(PRINT, TC01 ENTER cur=#<_current_tool> sel=#<_selected_tool>)

; Save the CALLER's active WCS before this macro touches it at all (selecting G59.3 below, over the puck) -
; restored (not hardcoded to G54) right before returning, so a mid-program tool change under any other WCS
; (Pocket etc under G59, say) comes back to the RIGHT origin afterward. #5220 = grblHAL's own "active
; coordinate system" named parameter (1=G54 .. 6=G59, 7=G59.1, 8=G59.2, 9=G59.3 - ngc_params.c coord_system).
; Confirmed on real hardware 2026-07-27: this used to hardcode G54 on return, which silently left Pocket's
; finishing pass running under the wrong WCS after its own mid-program tool change - its first move
; (computed in G59 coordinates, executed under G54) landed outside travel and hit a soft limit.
; (The capture itself moved to tlo.macro with the restore that consumed it - keeping a dead
;  #<_wcs> here would be one more thing to drift out of agreement with the macro that uses it.)

; Suspend any active G92 offset for the duration of this macro, and restore it before returning
; (G92.3, next to the WCS restore below). WCO = G5x offset + G92 offset + TLO, so a leftover G92
; shifts every non-G53 move this macro makes - including "G0 Z0" over the puck after G59.3 is
; selected, which is NOT a machine-coordinate move however much it reads like one.
;
; Confirmed on real hardware 2026-08-16: a persistent G92 of Z+37 (set by the Probing tab's G92
; coordinate-system option, and restored from NVS across resets because $384=0) made that G0 Z0
; target machine Z +37 against a Z envelope topping out at 0 -> ALARM:2 at TC02, before the probe.
; G49 below is NOT enough: it cancels the old tool's TLO and never touches G92.
; pcorner.macro and pvisecorner.macro already guard this - see their own notes about a stray G92
; landing a search ~70 mm from the real surface. This macro had the same exposure and no guard.
;
; G92.2/G92.3, NOT G92.1: this runs MID-JOB, and a job may legitimately be working under a G92.
; Destroying it here would move every remaining cut. G92.2 zeroes the applied offset while leaving
; the stored one intact; G92.3 puts it back. Both verified present in this controller's firmware
; (gcode.c: NonModal_ClearCoordinateOffset / NonModal_RestoreCoordinateOffset).
; ⚠️ G92.3 restores from NVS, so it depends on G92 persistence being ENABLED ($384=0, the default
; and this machine's setting). With $384=1 a G92 is never written, and G92.3 would restore a stale
; value instead of the caller's - if that setting is ever changed, revisit this pair.
G92.2

M5
M9
G53 G0 Z-5

o100 IF [#<_selected_tool> NE 8]
  ; Safe-Z goto to G30 (Z already lifted to -5 above): traverse XY at the safe
  ; height, then move Z. Decomposed by hand because grblHAL runs this macro
  ; directly - ioSender's "safe Z on Go To" only rewrites gotos it sends itself.
  ; #5181/#5182/#5183 = stored G30 machine position (ngc_params: g30_home).
  G53 G0 X#5181 Y#5182
  G53 G0 Z#5183
  ; (MSG,...) is grblHAL's own RS274NGC pending-M0-message mechanism - it does NOT run parameter
  ; substitution (only (PRINT,...) and (DEBUG,...) do, see ngc_expr.c ngc_process_comment/
  ; ngc_substitute_parameters) - a literal "#<_selected_tool>" was showing up unsubstituted in the
  ; prompt, confirmed on real hardware 2026-07-27 ("Swap to T#<_selected_tool>, press cycle start" with
  ; no actual number). (PRINT,...) DOES substitute, so it carries the real tool number instead; MSG is
  ; kept (without the number) for the actual M0 pending-message field.
  (PRINT, Swap to T#<_selected_tool>, press Run)
  (MSG, Swap tool, press Run)
  M0
  G49                ; cancel old tool's TLO before probing the new one
o100 ENDIF

; --- Reference this tool against the puck ---
; The whole sequence - park over the puck, pick the probe input, probe, restore the caller's WCS/G92,
; set or apply the TLO, $TLR - moved to tlo.macro on 2026-09-14 and is CALLED from here. It was
; extracted because the sender had grown a second copy of it, emitted inline into generated g-code,
; which had already drifted (Z-80 instead of Z-90, a hardcoded G54, an unconditional toolsetter
; select) and could not have been correct anyway: two of those three need o-word branching, and
; o-word flow control cannot be streamed. One implementation, on the controller, called by both.
;
; #<_tc_touchplate> and #<_tlo_ref> are already globals and are read directly by tlo.macro.
#<_tlo_toolid> = #<_selected_tool>
O<tlo> CALL

; Safe-Z goto to G30 (Z lifted just above): traverse XY at the safe height, then
; move Z. See the matching note on the G30 swap move earlier in this macro.
G53 G0 Z-5
G53 G0 X#5181 Y#5182
G53 G0 Z#5183
(PRINT, TC06 EXIT at G30)
